# ─── Third-party agent skills — machine-local, NOT committed ─────────────────
# `npx skills add <repo>` installs into .agents/skills/ inside the working tree,
# which would otherwise put megabytes of someone else's tooling into this PUBLIC
# repo and into every contributor's clone. skills-lock.json IS committed — it
# records the source repo and content hash of each installed skill, so an
# install is reproducible from the lock file without vendoring the payload.
#
# Ignoring is per-entry (`/*`, not `/`) so the HeroUI skills below can be
# re-included: git does not descend into a wholly-excluded directory, and a
# `!` negation inside one has no effect.
#
# The three HeroUI skills predate this rule, are first-party to our own UI
# stack, and are referenced by AGENTS.md / CLAUDE.md — they stay tracked.
# A genuinely project-owned skill added later needs its own `!` line here.
.agents/skills/*
!.agents/skills/heroui-migration/
!.agents/skills/heroui-native/
!.agents/skills/heroui-react/

# ─── HyperFrames video projects — machine-local, NOT committed ───────────────
# `videos/` holds HyperFrames composition projects. A single finished project
# measured 30 MB, of which ~28 MB was regenerable render output: a 4K mp4, a
# diagnostic mp4, and full-size verification/snapshot PNGs, plus thumbnail,
# waveform and media caches. Git keeps binaries in history for ever, so in a
# PUBLIC repo that cost lands on every clone permanently and cannot be undone
# without rewriting history.
#
# Ignored WHOLE rather than per-entry (unlike .agents/skills/ above): the
# source side of a project is small but the render output sits inside the same
# tree, so there is no clean `!` line to write. If a video's source ever needs
# to be shared, publish the finished mp4 somewhere built for binaries and keep
# the composition source in its own repo.
/videos/
